Harness

How to check an implementation against this corpus in an afternoon.
Everything here is a rule about a program you write; nothing here is
about Reticulum. For the bytes, start at doc/fields.


What a harness is

One program that takes the same arguments cmd/dump takes and prints the
same lines:

    my-harness <kind> <rawfile>

It reads the blobs from rawfile, hands them to the implementation, and
prints one field per line: the name in the first 18 columns, the value
after it. cmd/check runs it against every vector and diffs the output
against expect.

    DUMP=./my-harness ENCODE=no cmd/check

ENCODE=no skips the round trip, which rebuilds raw from expect. No
harness written so far implements it. It is worth having eventually,
because decoding proves only that an implementation reads what
python-rns writes, and it is not where to start.

    cmd/dump -l         the kinds a decoder can be asked for
    doc/fields          every field name, what it holds, which kinds
    README              one vector printed in full


Six rules

1. Print what the implementation computed.

   Not what the reference computed, and not what expect says. A field
   the implementation does not produce is what the corpus is for. If it
   has no hop limit, the harness has none either, the vector fails, and
   the disagreement is the result.

2. Where there is nothing to call, say so.

   Some implementations validate an announce behind one entry point
   that returns a boolean and never yields the fields. Following that
   code line by line in the harness is allowed, and marking the place is
   required, because such a field tests the transcription as much as the
   implementation:

       // Follows src/announce.js:26-49 with rns.js's own constants.

   Where the implementation has no such code at all, print "-". A dash
   fails the vector, and that is the honest outcome.

3. Exit 77 for a kind you have not implemented.

   Those vectors are reported as skipped and named, and are not counted
   against the result:

       40 passed, 9 failed, corpus 610068843, 1 skipped: ifac

   Nothing else may use that status. A harness that implements a kind
   and disagrees with the vector has found something, and that is the
   result.

   This is what makes a harness worth writing before it is finished.
   Four kinds on the first day is a result; the alternative, requiring
   every kind before anything can be measured, is why a new decoder
   starts with nothing to show.

4. One kind is a start.

   test/identity is a SHA-256 of 64 bytes, truncated to 16. Five
   vectors, four fields each, no packets and no cryptography beyond a
   hash. If the implementation exposes an identity at all, that harness
   is twenty lines and produces a real number.

   From there: keyset adds the two curve base multiplications,
   destination adds the name rules, announce adds the header and the
   signature, and by then most of the format is in hand.

5. An empty byte string is "-".

   Hex cannot spell one, and a name followed by nothing is a line that
   cannot be told from a truncated one. cmd/dump does this once, in
   field_hex, which is where to do it: one hex printer, one case.

   All six harnesses got it wrong and only one vector reaches it,
   test/destination/empty-aspect. Getting everything else right is not
   evidence that this is right.

6. An exception is not a field, and neither is a log line.

   A refusal is a result. Where the reference refuses an input, an
   implementation that refuses it agrees with the reference, and a
   harness that lets the exception out reports that agreement as a
   failure and invents a finding. Catch it and print "-" for the fields
   it gates. test/encrypted/low-order-ephemeral is that vector: no key
   agreement exists, and expect holds six dashes because of it.

   A library that logs to standard output writes into the field stream.
   Send its output to standard error before the first call; cmd/check
   reads standard output and nothing else.

   The general form: a field the implementation produced nothing for is
   "-", a field it produced the wrong value for is that wrong value,
   and neither may delete the fields after it. A harness that stops at
   the first defect measures one.


What to expect it to cost

The six harnesses in conformance/ run from 529 lines to 959, in six
languages, and every one of them covers announce and the link kinds.
The smallest, conformance/rns.js/dump.js, is the one to read first.

Most of that length is neither parsing nor cryptography. It is
reaching past an API that was written for an application rather than
for an observer: pulling a private key out of a struct that does not
export it, or reconstructing a link from a request packet because the
constructor wants a running instance. Budget for that rather than for
the protocol.


Reporting a result

CONFORMANCE holds one row per implementation, at a named commit, with
the harness that produced it in conformance/. A row is only reproducible
at that commit, so record it.

The rules for what goes in that directory, and how the existing
harnesses are built, are in conformance/README.

A result is not a verdict on an implementation. It is a count of
vectors, at one commit, through one harness, in the decode direction
only.
